Production Scenario: Deployment Successful but Application Down

What Happened

A new release was deployed to production using a CI/CD pipeline.
The pipeline completed successfully and monitoring showed that all application containers were running normally.

However, users immediately started facing 502 and 503 errors, and the application was not accessible.

From a system point of view, everything looked healthy, but from a user point of view, the system was down.


Why This Confused Everyone

●The DevOps team checked all the usual things:

●Deployment showed success

●Application containers were running

●No crashes or restarts

●Infrastructure was stable


Because all indicators were green, it was difficult to understand why production was failing.
This is a very common trap in real production environments.



The Real Problem (Root Cause)

●The issue was not with the deployment or infrastructure, but with how Kubernetes decided when the application was ready to receive traffic.

●The system assumed the application was ready as soon as the container started, but in reality:

●The application still needed time to connect to the database

●Background services were still initializing

●Cache and dependencies were not ready

●Even though the container was running, the application itself was not ready.


What Went Wrong Conceptually

●There is an important difference between:

●Application started

●Application ready for users

●The system treated these two as the same thing.

As a result, user traffic was sent to the application too early, causing failures and errors.



Why This Happens in Production

●Many applications respond with a “success” status even when:

●Internal dependencies are not available

●Startup tasks are incomplete

●Required services are still warming up


Because of this, traffic reaches the application before it is actually capable of handling requests.


How the Issue Was Identified

●The team closely reviewed application behavior during startup and noticed that:

●Errors appeared only during the first few minutes after deployment

●After some time, the application became stable without any changes

●Restarting pods temporarily fixed the issue

●This pattern pointed to a startup readiness problem, not a code bug.


The Fix

●The solution was to ensure that traffic is sent only after the application is truly ready.

This involved:

●Clearly defining what “ready” means for the application

●Waiting for database connections and required services

●Allowing enough startup time before accepting user requests

●Once traffic was delayed until full readiness, the errors disappeared.


Final Outcome
After the fix:

■ Users stopped seeing errors

■ Deployments became reliable

■ No rollbacks were needed

■ Confidence in the deployment process increased
